>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `clasetLib`

``` hol4
structure clasetLib
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Maintains the shared rule set used by classical and Aesop proof tactics.

A stored rule is classified by its use and, where applicable, by whether
applying it is safe for proof search:

| Attribute | Theorem-list marker | Classification |
|---|---|---|
| `[sintro]` | `SIntro th` | safe introduction |
| `[intro]` or `[intro=NN]` | `Intro th` | unsafe introduction |
| `[selim]` | `SElim th` | safe elimination |
| `[elim]` or `[elim=NN]` | `Elim th` | unsafe elimination |
| `[sdest]` | `SDest th` | safe destruction |
| `[dest]` or `[dest=NN]` | `Dest th` | unsafe destruction |
| `[sforward]` | `SForward th` | safe forward reasoning |
| `[forward]` or `[forward=NN]` | `Forward th` | unsafe forward reasoning |
| `[norm]` or `[norm=k]` | `Norm th` | Aesop normalisation |
| `[aesop_simp]` | `Simp th` | Aesop simplification |
| none | `Del "name"` | remove a rule for one invocation |

Introduction rules match their conclusion against a goal and turn their
premises into subgoals. Elimination rules use a major premise matched
against an assumption. Destruction rules are converted internally to
elimination form. Forward rules derive and add a new assumption from an
existing one.

Normalisation and Aesop simplification rules run in the normalisation phase
of [`aesopLib.AESOP_TAC`](#aesopLib.AESOP_TAC). Here *safe* describes
proof-search behaviour, not logical soundness: every rule is a kernel
theorem.

### Persistent theorem attributes

Plain introduction, elimination, destruction, and forward attributes
denote unsafe rules. Their optional numeric argument is an estimated
success percentage from 1 through 100. Aesop uses the percentage to order
best-first search; omission gives the default 50 percent. Attribute
arguments use the `attribute=value` syntax shown here:

``` hol4
Theorem implication_intro[intro=75]:
  p ==> q ==> p /\ q
Proof
  ...
QED
```

The safe attributes `[sintro]`, `[selim]`, `[sdest]`, and `[sforward]`
take no argument. `[norm]` has penalty 0; `[norm=k]` supplies an integer
penalty. Lower penalties run first, with negative penalties before the
built-in normalisation rules and positive penalties after them.

The argument-free `[aesop_simp]` attribute adds a theorem to Aesop's
persistent rewrite set. This set extends `srw_ss()` for Aesop
normalisation; it does not add the theorem to `srw_ss()` itself.

All these declarations are inherited through theory ancestry. The usual
`local` theorem modifier makes a declaration local, as in
`[intro=75,local]`. Rule removal is provided by `delrule` and
`temp_delrule`, not by a removal attribute. Stored rules are normally
named by their fully qualified database names, such as
`bool$AND_INTRO_THM`.

The base claset also derives safe datatype rules from `TypeBase`.
Constructor distinctness contributes elimination rules in both
orientations. Constructor injectivity contributes both directions of each
equivalence: a safe introduction rule for rebuilding constructor equality
from component equalities and a safe destruction rule for extracting
component equalities. These derived facts follow datatypes added after
library load as well as entries found during the initial catch-up.

### Per-invocation markers

The marker constructors modify only the temporary rule set assembled for
a tactic call. `SIntro`, `Intro`, `SElim`, `Elim`, `SDest`, and `Dest`
add the enclosed theorem with the corresponding classical classification.
`Norm`, `Forward`, and `SForward` provide the Aesop-only classifications.
The marker forms do not carry numeric arguments: `Norm` uses penalty 0 and
`Forward` uses the default 50 percent. `Del` removes a named rule. The
original theorem and global rule set are unchanged.

An unmarked theorem supplied to a classical, tableau, or Aesop tactic is
inserted into the goal as an assumption. Content-bearing generic
simplifier wrappers such as `Once th` are unwrapped and inserted, while
inert generic controls such as `Cong`, `Excl` and `SF` are ignored.
Explicit rule roles therefore require a corresponding rule marker.

Insertion is `ASSUME_TAC`, so a theorem carrying hypotheses contributes them
to the proved theorem whether or not the search uses it, exactly as
`ASSUME_TAC` and `MP_TAC` do elsewhere. That is what makes
`FIRST_ASSUM (fn th => AUTO_TAC [th])` valid, its hypothesis already being
an assumption of the goal; a theorem whose hypotheses are not assumptions of
the goal makes the invocation fail under `VALID`. Supply such a theorem
under a rule marker instead, and its hypotheses reach the result only if the
rule fires.

Clasimp and Aesop theorem lists additionally recognize `Simp th`, which
adds only a temporary rewrite, and `Iff th`, which adds temporary rewrite
and classical views. Classical-only and tableau tactics reject those two
markers because they have no simpset.

### Failure

Malformed rules may be rejected when an attribute or per-invocation marker
is processed. Percentages outside 1 through 100, malformed integer
penalties, and arguments on safe attributes are rejected.

### Example

The seed conjunction rule can be suppressed for one tactic invocation:

``` hol4
TRY (classicalLib.SAFE_TAC
       [clasetLib.Del "bool$AND_INTRO_THM"])
```

The deletion does not affect later tactic calls.

